gl: Keep the GLES2 fallback on Linux

Upstream deprecated GLES2 in M146 (crbug.com/444049511): when an OpenGL
ES 3.0 context cannot be created, the GPU process no longer retries ES 2.0
but gives up on hardware acceleration entirely, dropping to software
compositing with no WebGL (the automatic SwiftShader fallback is also
deprecated). On VideoCore IV devices (Pi 0-3), whose vc4 driver correctly
reports OpenGL ES 2.0 only, this silently removes all hardware
acceleration.

Until M150 the behaviour was governed by kFallbackToSWIfGLES3NotSupported,
which we disabled by default; from M151 to M155 we overrode the hard-coded
Linux branch of ShouldFallbackToSWIfGLES3NotSupported(). M156 deleted the
feature and that function (crbug.com/555664323) and inlined
allow_es_version_fallback = false at its only two callers: the GPU info
collection context and the passthrough decoder's ES3/WebGL2 context. Set
both back to true, restoring the pre-M146 ES 2.0 fallback.

allow_es_version_fallback is only consulted when an ES3 context cannot be
created (gl_context_egl.cc), so ES3-capable devices (v3d on Pi 4/5) are
unaffected.
--- a/gpu/command_buffer/service/service_utils.cc
+++ b/gpu/command_buffer/service/service_utils.cc
@@ -157,7 +157,11 @@ gl::GLContextAttribs GenerateGLContextAt
     if (IsWebGL2OrES3ContextType(context_type)) {
       attribs.client_major_es_version = 3;
       attribs.client_minor_es_version = 0;
-      attribs.allow_es_version_fallback = false;
+      // Raspberry Pi: keep the ES 2.0 fallback. On GLES2-only VideoCore IV
+      // devices (Pi 0-3) disallowing it replaces hardware ES 2.0 with software
+      // rendering. Only consulted when ES3 context creation fails, so
+      // ES3-capable hardware (v3d on Pi 4/5) is unaffected.
+      attribs.allow_es_version_fallback = true;
     } else {
       DCHECK(IsWebGL1OrES2ContextType(context_type));
       attribs.client_major_es_version = 2;
